1
00:00:02,868 --> 00:00:04,837
BEAR: Deserts.

2
00:00:05,237 --> 00:00:09,142
Scorched, desolate, hostile.

3
00:00:11,277 --> 00:00:14,014
But, alive.

4
00:00:21,187 --> 00:00:23,723
One million hungry bats

5
00:00:23,823 --> 00:00:29,029
forced to leave the safety of
their cave before nightfall.

6
00:00:37,804 --> 00:00:40,106
The sun is so powerful here,

7
00:00:40,206 --> 00:00:43,375
there's barely anything to eat.

8
00:00:43,809 --> 00:00:48,715
The bats can only catch enough
insects by leaving early.

9
00:00:50,583 --> 00:00:55,088
But, in daylight, they're
dangerously exposed.

10
00:00:59,258 --> 00:01:02,295
Hawks wait for them.

11
00:01:39,365 --> 00:01:43,336
In the desert,
timing is everything.

12
00:01:46,039 --> 00:01:49,809
Get it wrong and
you pay the price.

13
00:01:57,683 --> 00:02:01,588
The sun governs the
rhythm of desert life.

14
00:02:02,789 --> 00:02:05,324
And the animals that
make their home here

15
00:02:05,424 --> 00:02:08,628
are at the mercy of its power.

16
00:02:11,296 --> 00:02:17,237
(theme music plays).

17
00:02:23,308 --> 00:02:28,248
Above me is a nuclear reactor
almost a million miles across.

18
00:02:31,117 --> 00:02:35,054
Every second, the sun spits
out more energy than humans

19
00:02:35,154 --> 00:02:38,158
have used in all our history.

20
00:02:39,826 --> 00:02:44,664
And nowhere is its power more
potent than in the desert.

21
00:02:52,505 --> 00:02:56,242
Amazingly, there are animals
that spend their lives

22
00:02:56,342 --> 00:02:59,179
in this scorching heat.

23
00:03:02,848 --> 00:03:07,720
But now the hottest places on
Earth are getting even hotter.

24
00:03:10,423 --> 00:03:13,559
And even the most resilient
creatures are facing a fight

25
00:03:13,659 --> 00:03:16,362
like never before.

26
00:03:35,281 --> 00:03:39,117
The sun's rule begins.

27
00:03:39,885 --> 00:03:44,457
From now on, survival
gets tougher by the hour.

28
00:03:57,670 --> 00:04:00,906
Nubian ibex make their
home in the highest,

29
00:04:01,006 --> 00:04:04,177
steepest corners
of the desert.

30
00:04:05,111 --> 00:04:08,548
A daunting place
to raise two kids.

31
00:04:10,750 --> 00:04:15,021
(bleating).

32
00:04:26,866 --> 00:04:30,970
But, their greatest
challenge comes from the sun.

33
00:04:34,240 --> 00:04:36,309
In the last few decades,

34
00:04:36,409 --> 00:04:39,979
heatwaves have
become more severe.

35
00:04:40,913 --> 00:04:46,219
They need water, but it
lies 1000 feet below.

36
00:04:52,358 --> 00:04:57,230
(bleating).

37
00:05:09,341 --> 00:05:12,111
(bleating).

38
00:05:19,518 --> 00:05:24,290
It's a perilous journey for
kids only a few days old.

39
00:05:29,595 --> 00:05:33,866
But, their soft soled hooves
grip like climbing shoes.

40
00:05:35,768 --> 00:05:39,806
And, they appear
utterly immune to vertigo.

41
00:05:53,652 --> 00:05:58,958
(bleating).

42
00:06:06,131 --> 00:06:10,603
Little grows on these
steep, sun baked slopes.

43
00:06:13,872 --> 00:06:18,378
All that's underfoot
is crumbling rock.

44
00:06:36,295 --> 00:06:42,502
♪ ♪

45
00:06:51,811 --> 00:06:56,048
(bleating).

46
00:06:57,149 --> 00:06:59,585
Driven downward by the sun,

47
00:06:59,685 --> 00:07:03,089
the family's journey
is almost over.

48
00:07:05,224 --> 00:07:07,993
But now, they no longer
enjoy the protection

49
00:07:08,093 --> 00:07:10,997
of the high cliffs.

50
00:07:24,109 --> 00:07:26,946
(bleating).

51
00:07:28,814 --> 00:07:31,617
An Arabian wolf.

52
00:07:31,950 --> 00:07:35,555
He outweighs an
ibex kid ten to one.

53
00:07:42,428 --> 00:07:47,366
In this desert, he's the
ibex's most feared predator.

54
00:07:56,808 --> 00:07:59,846
By keeping her family up slope,

55
00:08:03,182 --> 00:08:06,052
she has a slender advantage.

56
00:08:08,821 --> 00:08:12,058
But falling rocks
won't stop him.

57
00:08:45,024 --> 00:08:47,860
(bleating).

58
00:08:56,602 --> 00:08:59,438
(bleating).

59
00:08:59,538 --> 00:09:02,642
The kids have
unbeatable agility,

60
00:09:08,480 --> 00:09:11,117
but lack experience.

61
00:09:24,530 --> 00:09:27,800
(bleating).

62
00:09:50,089 --> 00:09:53,893
There's nothing
the mother can do.

63
00:10:11,377 --> 00:10:14,847
Now, she will focus all
her energy on protecting

64
00:10:14,947 --> 00:10:18,351
and providing for
her remaining kid.

65
00:10:20,987 --> 00:10:24,857
Deep in the canyon,
sheltered from the sun,

66
00:10:26,558 --> 00:10:29,228
it's a different world.

67
00:10:33,532 --> 00:10:37,570
This is what they've
risked everything for.

68
00:10:42,674 --> 00:10:46,278
The desert's most
precious resource.

69
00:10:49,782 --> 00:10:52,251
A lifeline.

70
00:10:52,952 --> 00:10:55,921
But a costly one.

71
00:11:02,694 --> 00:11:08,334
As the sun climbs higher
its power intensifies,

72
00:11:09,702 --> 00:11:13,706
tightening its
grip on desert life.

73
00:11:16,542 --> 00:11:19,177
In some places,
it seems miraculous

74
00:11:19,277 --> 00:11:22,782
that anything can survive.

75
00:11:25,751 --> 00:11:29,021
The temperature here is
rising three times faster

76
00:11:29,121 --> 00:11:32,058
than the global average.

77
00:11:39,397 --> 00:11:44,603
This mining town was
abandoned over 60 years ago.

78
00:11:54,246 --> 00:11:57,883
Some believe these
buildings are haunted.

79
00:12:06,825 --> 00:12:09,929
But whatever's stalking
this ghost town

80
00:12:13,164 --> 00:12:15,768
is very much alive.

81
00:12:23,175 --> 00:12:26,145
BEAR: In Africa's
Namib Desert...

82
00:12:27,679 --> 00:12:31,350
A brown hyena has set
up home in a ghost town.

83
00:12:46,097 --> 00:12:48,701
Her pups are hungry.

84
00:12:52,471 --> 00:12:55,407
And, finding food in
this desolate landscape

85
00:12:55,507 --> 00:12:58,844
seems impossible.

86
00:13:03,682 --> 00:13:07,852
Yet, less than a mile away,
the desert meets one of the

87
00:13:07,952 --> 00:13:11,957
richest seas in the world.

88
00:13:28,540 --> 00:13:31,744
The Atlantic brings
life to the Namib.

89
00:13:32,644 --> 00:13:36,048
Thousands of cape fur seals.

90
00:13:37,816 --> 00:13:41,287
Pups could be an easy target.

91
00:13:44,556 --> 00:13:48,293
15 pound balls of blubber,

92
00:13:48,393 --> 00:13:51,664
totally unsuited to desert life.

93
00:13:57,569 --> 00:14:01,540
Black backed jackals
are also on the hunt.

94
00:14:03,908 --> 00:14:09,715
(seal calls).

95
00:14:13,919 --> 00:14:17,423
But, the pups have
formidable bodyguards.

96
00:14:18,590 --> 00:14:23,062
(growling).

97
00:14:24,930 --> 00:14:26,765
In a colony this size,

98
00:14:26,865 --> 00:14:30,236
there are plenty of
other opportunities.

99
00:14:32,838 --> 00:14:38,911
(growling).

100
00:14:46,584 --> 00:14:50,222
A placenta from
a recent birth.

101
00:14:52,958 --> 00:14:55,427
With three pups of
her own to feed,

102
00:14:55,527 --> 00:14:58,964
the hyena needs a bigger meal.

103
00:15:01,266 --> 00:15:04,903
(seal calls).

104
00:15:05,003 --> 00:15:10,142
As the land heats up,
bodyguards retreat to the sea.

105
00:15:35,967 --> 00:15:37,936
Armed with sharp teeth,

106
00:15:38,036 --> 00:15:41,339
even a lone pup can
put up a brave fight.

107
00:15:41,640 --> 00:15:46,712
(squeaky growling).

108
00:15:50,815 --> 00:15:54,886
(squeaky growling).

109
00:15:55,553 --> 00:16:00,159
It's easier to wait until
the sun takes its toll.

110
00:16:08,000 --> 00:16:11,402
Disoriented by
the punishing heat,

111
00:16:11,503 --> 00:16:15,341
this pup strays
into the desert.

112
00:16:26,417 --> 00:16:32,257
(squeaking, crying).

113
00:16:35,760 --> 00:16:39,431
(squeaking, crying).

114
00:16:54,379 --> 00:16:58,684
She timed her
hunt to perfection.

115
00:17:09,761 --> 00:17:14,299
Now it's time to relax
during the heat of the day.

116
00:17:18,971 --> 00:17:22,541
Even now, with
temperatures still rising,

117
00:17:22,641 --> 00:17:25,878
not all animals
can afford to rest.

118
00:17:36,421 --> 00:17:40,559
These meerkat pups are
just five weeks old.

119
00:17:44,296 --> 00:17:48,133
They still have a lot to
learn about desert survival.

120
00:17:50,902 --> 00:17:53,238
It's a dry season.

121
00:17:53,338 --> 00:17:56,809
Rain is six months away.

122
00:18:00,078 --> 00:18:04,850
And the closest river
hasn't flowed in seven years.

123
00:18:12,924 --> 00:18:16,194
Finding food is the
toughest challenge.

124
00:18:16,929 --> 00:18:20,933
Most of it's hiding
under sun-baked soil.

125
00:18:23,101 --> 00:18:28,507
With adults on guard
duty scanning for danger.

126
00:18:30,442 --> 00:18:33,612
Ready to sound the alarm.

127
00:18:33,946 --> 00:18:37,216
it's safe for the
pups' lessons to begin.

128
00:18:44,289 --> 00:18:46,792
First, digging.

129
00:18:49,961 --> 00:18:52,965
Harder than it looks.

130
00:18:54,232 --> 00:18:58,704
An adult can dig its own
body weight every few seconds.

131
00:19:00,271 --> 00:19:04,176
But, for a pup,
it's easier to beg.

132
00:19:05,043 --> 00:19:10,482
(barking).

133
00:19:11,883 --> 00:19:16,989
(chattering).

134
00:19:19,457 --> 00:19:23,629
Why not let the
adults do the hard work?

135
00:19:29,000 --> 00:19:32,404
But, not all handouts
make for a simple meal.

136
00:19:34,306 --> 00:19:36,474
A scorpion.

137
00:19:36,575 --> 00:19:40,244
The adult has already
disabled its stinger,

138
00:19:40,345 --> 00:19:43,916
but its pincers are
still fully functional.

139
00:19:50,488 --> 00:19:53,225
Learning how to deal
with dangerous animals

140
00:19:53,325 --> 00:19:56,361
is a vital desert skill.

141
00:20:01,166 --> 00:20:04,836
All too soon, it's
time for a break.

142
00:20:06,438 --> 00:20:10,242
They need to find
shade in the searing sun.

143
00:20:16,781 --> 00:20:20,285
But, lessons aren't over yet.

144
00:20:27,459 --> 00:20:31,597
(hissing).

145
00:20:36,301 --> 00:20:39,237
BEAR: In Africa's
Kalahari Desert,

146
00:20:39,971 --> 00:20:44,476
a young meerkat is face
to face with a cape cobra.

147
00:20:48,480 --> 00:20:52,417
One on one, he's
in big trouble.

148
00:20:54,653 --> 00:20:57,122
But when his family
hear the call,

149
00:20:57,222 --> 00:21:01,159
(squealing).

150
00:21:01,259 --> 00:21:03,996
they race to the rescue.

151
00:21:06,732 --> 00:21:10,168
(hissing).

152
00:21:12,570 --> 00:21:16,474
This will be one of the
most important lessons of all.

153
00:21:16,808 --> 00:21:20,412
How to handle a
venomous cobra.

154
00:21:20,512 --> 00:21:24,249
(hissing, chattering).

155
00:21:25,583 --> 00:21:28,820
If they can't see the
snake off their territory,

156
00:21:28,920 --> 00:21:32,524
the entire family
remains at risk.

157
00:21:41,233 --> 00:21:47,506
(hissing, chattering).

158
00:21:47,606 --> 00:21:54,079
(hissing, chattering).

159
00:22:01,586 --> 00:22:04,022
The pups now know,

160
00:22:04,122 --> 00:22:08,593
their secret to living in
this inhospitable desert,

161
00:22:10,595 --> 00:22:12,564
teamwork.

162
00:22:18,403 --> 00:22:21,673
Midday is approaching.

163
00:22:25,177 --> 00:22:29,147
School's over and it's
time to take shelter.

164
00:22:34,385 --> 00:22:39,658
For the next few hours, the
sun's full power is unleashed.

165
00:22:44,229 --> 00:22:48,333
Very few animals can
survive out in the open.

166
00:22:56,708 --> 00:23:00,212
This is the
world's oldest desert.

167
00:23:01,413 --> 00:23:06,084
It's existed for at
least 55 million years.

168
00:23:06,384 --> 00:23:10,489
And now the temperature
is rising even higher.

169
00:23:12,657 --> 00:23:16,495
An extra degree or two a
century may not sound much.

170
00:23:17,996 --> 00:23:22,234
But where creatures are
already pushed to the limit

171
00:23:24,669 --> 00:23:27,305
it could be fatal.

172
00:23:33,745 --> 00:23:38,483
At midday, the sand is hot
enough to melt your shoes.

173
00:23:44,822 --> 00:23:47,092
The shovel snouted lizard

174
00:23:47,192 --> 00:23:51,663
avoids burning its
feet by dancing.

175
00:23:55,533 --> 00:24:02,040
♪ ♪

176
00:24:20,524 --> 00:24:23,862
The buck spoor spider can
survive higher temperatures

177
00:24:23,962 --> 00:24:26,798
than any other spider.

178
00:24:28,600 --> 00:24:31,570
Up to 120 Fahrenheit.

179
00:24:36,607 --> 00:24:40,044
But it's gonna get
hotter than that.

180
00:24:40,879 --> 00:24:44,549
She must work fast,
weaving a blanket of sand

181
00:24:44,649 --> 00:24:47,619
to cover the entrance
to her burrow,

182
00:24:49,654 --> 00:24:52,824
where she escapes the heat.

183
00:25:00,999 --> 00:25:06,204
Now, the sand
hits 160 degrees.

184
00:25:15,380 --> 00:25:20,385
Yet, for hotrod ants, it's
the start of the working day.

185
00:25:23,087 --> 00:25:27,726
Alone on the dunes, they
scavenge the sun's victims.

186
00:25:33,397 --> 00:25:37,235
Long legs are a lifesaver.

187
00:25:37,769 --> 00:25:42,340
Just a fifth of an inch
up is 12 degrees cooler.

188
00:25:44,008 --> 00:25:46,678
And, the hotrods
live up to their name,

189
00:25:46,778 --> 00:25:50,682
running so fast they
create a cooling breeze.

190
00:25:57,355 --> 00:26:01,993
When it gets too much, they
climb off the sand entirely.

191
00:26:05,263 --> 00:26:08,266
By pushing the limits
of thermal survival,

192
00:26:08,366 --> 00:26:12,704
the ants can be active when
their predators are in hiding.

193
00:26:16,374 --> 00:26:19,778
But, even they must get
back to the nest soon.

194
00:26:23,847 --> 00:26:27,285
Before they boil to death.

195
00:26:45,470 --> 00:26:49,407
The ants' struggles
won't go unnoticed.

196
00:26:50,408 --> 00:26:54,312
Vibrations travel
down silk threads,

197
00:26:54,412 --> 00:26:57,849
to an underground lair.

198
00:27:08,259 --> 00:27:14,666
♪ ♪

199
00:27:27,145 --> 00:27:32,951
Sometimes, the spoor spider
lets the sun do its killing.

200
00:27:45,529 --> 00:27:50,101
It will emerge later to
eat its precooked meal.

201
00:27:55,540 --> 00:27:58,176
Across the world's deserts,

202
00:27:58,276 --> 00:28:02,080
temperature peak in
the early afternoon.

203
00:28:06,617 --> 00:28:09,153
With barely a trace of life,

204
00:28:09,254 --> 00:28:12,891
these landscapes look
like the surface of Mars.

205
00:28:16,627 --> 00:28:21,633
From space, the full beauty
of desert geology is revealed.

206
00:28:25,136 --> 00:28:30,575
In Africa, the eye of the
Sahara is 30 miles wide.

207
00:28:33,711 --> 00:28:38,283
A collapsed dome of rock
millions of years old.

208
00:28:41,152 --> 00:28:45,023
Landscapes like this will
become more common if deserts

209
00:28:45,123 --> 00:28:49,160
continue to expand
in our warming world.

210
00:28:57,501 --> 00:29:01,372
Right now, the closest we
have to a desert continent

211
00:29:01,472 --> 00:29:04,208
is Australia.

212
00:29:04,308 --> 00:29:08,246
The driest of all
inhabited land masses.

213
00:29:16,588 --> 00:29:19,323
A brutal drought has
hit an area the size of

214
00:29:19,423 --> 00:29:23,428
California and Montana combined.

215
00:29:26,263 --> 00:29:31,369
In places, under an inch of
rain has fallen in a year.

216
00:29:33,938 --> 00:29:37,842
Right now, survival
is all that matters.

217
00:29:44,115 --> 00:29:46,517
Unlike smaller animals,

218
00:29:46,617 --> 00:29:50,755
red kangaroos can't
burrow to escape the heat.

219
00:29:52,356 --> 00:29:55,360
Instead, they have evolved
to be some of the toughest

220
00:29:55,460 --> 00:29:58,797
of all desert creatures.

221
00:30:03,535 --> 00:30:08,807
But at 115 degrees, even
they begin to struggle.

222
00:30:11,309 --> 00:30:15,613
This mother and her joey
are dangerously overheating.

223
00:30:18,783 --> 00:30:22,253
They must find
shelter and fast.

224
00:30:32,463 --> 00:30:35,233
BEAR: In Australia, a
kangaroo and her joey

225
00:30:35,333 --> 00:30:38,936
are dangerously overheating.

226
00:30:39,637 --> 00:30:43,508
They have one
chance to escape.

227
00:30:58,723 --> 00:31:05,063
♪ ♪

228
00:31:09,067 --> 00:31:13,071
Shade cuts the
sun's energy by 80%.

229
00:31:19,009 --> 00:31:22,413
But latecomers
are not welcome.

230
00:31:26,917 --> 00:31:29,186
In the full force of the sun,

231
00:31:29,287 --> 00:31:33,391
this mother must draw on
all her survival skills.

232
00:31:36,260 --> 00:31:40,031
Digging offers a
little relief.

233
00:31:41,799 --> 00:31:44,102
Just five inches underground,

234
00:31:44,202 --> 00:31:48,206
the soil can be
50 degrees cooler.

235
00:31:51,209 --> 00:31:55,947
As a last resort, she uses
precious water reserves.

236
00:31:58,483 --> 00:32:01,719
Evaporating saliva cools
the blood in capillaries

237
00:32:01,819 --> 00:32:05,156
beneath her skin.

238
00:32:07,024 --> 00:32:10,428
But the heat keeps rising.

239
00:32:12,296 --> 00:32:14,799
Australia receives
more solar energy

240
00:32:14,899 --> 00:32:18,069
than any other landmass.

241
00:32:36,920 --> 00:32:40,558
They barely have
the strength to move.

242
00:32:44,428 --> 00:32:48,232
And, with almost
nothing left to eat

243
00:32:50,101 --> 00:32:53,871
they're all slowly starving.

244
00:33:04,915 --> 00:33:10,254
In the most severe droughts,
80% of red kangaroos can die.

245
00:33:28,105 --> 00:33:33,478
Their bodies quickly mummify
in the dry, desert air.

246
00:33:38,249 --> 00:33:41,785
Yet, a few of the very
toughest kangaroos

247
00:33:41,885 --> 00:33:45,723
somehow manage to survive.

248
00:33:49,126 --> 00:33:52,596
When the rains finally
come, their offspring will

249
00:33:52,696 --> 00:33:56,134
repopulate the outback.

250
00:34:01,405 --> 00:34:05,910
Not all deserts are
so desperately bleak.

251
00:34:18,656 --> 00:34:24,896
Saguaro cacti can tower over
60 feet and weigh two tons.

252
00:34:28,666 --> 00:34:33,304
Just one of over 2,000
plant species that grow here.

253
00:34:37,241 --> 00:34:41,446
In this green, spiky desert.

254
00:34:45,516 --> 00:34:49,187
A young Harris
hawk has a problem.

255
00:34:49,987 --> 00:34:52,623
(cawing).

256
00:34:53,090 --> 00:34:57,061
There are lots of
places for his prey to hide.

257
00:34:57,494 --> 00:34:59,130
(cawing).

258
00:35:05,269 --> 00:35:07,738
(cawing).

259
00:35:08,772 --> 00:35:13,277
It forces Harris hawks to
hunt like no other bird.

260
00:35:18,382 --> 00:35:21,352
In a pack.

261
00:35:31,529 --> 00:35:35,365
And, now the sun's dropping.

262
00:35:36,133 --> 00:35:39,470
It's cool enough to
launch an attack.

263
00:35:44,942 --> 00:35:47,144
As the youngest family member,

264
00:35:47,244 --> 00:35:51,215
his job is to flush the
rabbit into the open.

265
00:35:59,690 --> 00:36:03,060
Above, his mother
coordinates the hunt.

266
00:36:04,896 --> 00:36:08,665
The rest of the family
take their positions,

267
00:36:08,765 --> 00:36:12,069
ready for the chase.

268
00:36:25,249 --> 00:36:30,221
A dive signals
that the hunt is on.

269
00:36:36,060 --> 00:36:38,496
The three chasers
enter the game.

270
00:36:38,596 --> 00:36:42,833
(screaming).

271
00:36:54,612 --> 00:36:58,716
After a miss,
another chaser takes over.

272
00:37:18,535 --> 00:37:22,440
Finally, it's the
youngster's turn.

273
00:37:28,545 --> 00:37:30,748
(screaming).

274
00:37:35,852 --> 00:37:38,456
Beating his wings
four times a second,

275
00:37:38,556 --> 00:37:42,526
he accelerates to
over 30 miles an hour.

276
00:37:52,903 --> 00:37:56,507
(cawing).

277
00:38:02,146 --> 00:38:04,448
By working as a team,

278
00:38:04,548 --> 00:38:08,052
the hawks more than double
their chances of success.

279
00:38:10,855 --> 00:38:13,124
A smart hunting strategy

280
00:38:13,224 --> 00:38:16,460
in a desert with so
many places to hide.

281
00:38:24,301 --> 00:38:27,071
The Sonoran is the
most diverse desert

282
00:38:27,171 --> 00:38:30,308
in the United States.

283
00:38:32,409 --> 00:38:36,147
But, the monsoonal
storms that bring it life

284
00:38:39,482 --> 00:38:42,620
also spawn monsters.

285
00:38:55,032 --> 00:38:58,635
BEAR: On summer afternoons
in the western United States,

286
00:38:59,203 --> 00:39:02,339
the sun whips up winds
that suck moist air

287
00:39:02,439 --> 00:39:05,576
into the desert.

288
00:39:12,849 --> 00:39:16,620
Storms bring life to the land.

289
00:39:19,423 --> 00:39:23,394
(thunder, lightning).

290
00:39:25,029 --> 00:39:29,066
But, sometimes the
winds generate a monster.

291
00:39:33,437 --> 00:39:36,907
Moving at 50 miles an hour.

292
00:39:38,208 --> 00:39:41,712
A dust storm can engulf
and even suffocate

293
00:39:41,812 --> 00:39:44,882
the animals in its path.

294
00:39:51,555 --> 00:39:57,294
An unstoppable manifestation
of the sun's destructive power.

295
00:40:09,239 --> 00:40:14,278
By late afternoon, deserts
finally become less hostile.

296
00:40:16,346 --> 00:40:20,418
Conditions are
calmer and cooler.

297
00:40:24,321 --> 00:40:28,125
The perfect time for a hunter.

298
00:40:33,997 --> 00:40:36,033
It may be the end of the day,

299
00:40:36,133 --> 00:40:40,371
but this caracal is
just getting started.

300
00:40:50,847 --> 00:40:55,352
The prey she's after are all
coming home for the night.

301
00:41:00,223 --> 00:41:05,362
Hundreds of sociable weaver
birds return to roost in their

302
00:41:05,462 --> 00:41:07,965
enormous communal nest.

303
00:41:09,633 --> 00:41:14,038
(chirping).

304
00:41:16,974 --> 00:41:20,744
(chirping).

305
00:41:24,047 --> 00:41:27,318
(chirping).

306
00:41:28,085 --> 00:41:32,156
Muscular legs
propel her into the air.

307
00:41:35,826 --> 00:41:40,097
And, with claws splayed
like grappling hooks

308
00:41:41,331 --> 00:41:45,603
she'll try to catch any
bird within reach.

309
00:41:47,337 --> 00:41:52,743
(chirping).

310
00:41:55,179 --> 00:41:59,750
She can leap 11
feet vertically.

311
00:42:09,092 --> 00:42:11,995
(chirping).

312
00:42:12,096 --> 00:42:16,667
This could be her last
chance before sunset.

313
00:42:25,709 --> 00:42:28,579
(chirping).

314
00:42:33,450 --> 00:42:37,555
Deserts seldom give up
their scarce resources.

315
00:42:42,593 --> 00:42:46,764
But, the caracal will continue
hunting through the night.

316
00:42:49,666 --> 00:42:53,103
She's just one of many
species that use the ultimate

317
00:42:53,203 --> 00:42:57,207
survival strategy
against the sun.

318
00:42:59,543 --> 00:43:04,548
Timing their lives to
avoid it almost entirely.

319
00:43:15,358 --> 00:43:17,427
It's only at night that

320
00:43:17,527 --> 00:43:21,332
the true magic of
deserts is revealed.

321
00:43:23,400 --> 00:43:27,371
These fennec foxes have
been holed up for the day,

322
00:43:27,871 --> 00:43:30,741
waiting for darkness.

323
00:43:35,679 --> 00:43:39,917
Just to catch a glimpse
of them is extremely rare.

324
00:43:45,255 --> 00:43:49,093
Let alone to see two pups.

325
00:43:57,601 --> 00:44:01,971
They've emerged into a
world bustling with creatures,

326
00:44:02,071 --> 00:44:06,944
adapted to a nocturnal life in
our planet's largest dessert.

327
00:44:10,414 --> 00:44:14,951
Dense fur insulates the
fennecs against the cold.

328
00:44:15,819 --> 00:44:19,823
A reflective retina
enhances night vision.

329
00:44:20,357 --> 00:44:25,462
And, enormous ears
pinpoint the slightest sound.

330
00:44:26,196 --> 00:44:28,365
(owl hoots).

331
00:44:31,534 --> 00:44:35,606
The fact that such a diversity
of animals can thrive here in

332
00:44:35,706 --> 00:44:39,509
a landscape that at
first seems uninhabitable,

333
00:44:39,609 --> 00:44:42,179
is remarkable.

334
00:44:54,223 --> 00:44:57,294
Yet, for all nocturnal
desert animals,

335
00:44:57,394 --> 00:45:00,831
life is a race against time.

336
00:45:01,698 --> 00:45:06,103
Soon enough, the
sun will rise again.

337
00:45:24,287 --> 00:45:28,458
With each new day, life
here gets more challenging.

338
00:45:28,958 --> 00:45:31,461
As global temperatures
rise ever higher,

339
00:45:31,561 --> 00:45:34,331
our deserts are
expanding by almost

340
00:45:34,431 --> 00:45:38,569
50,000 square miles every year.

341
00:45:40,837 --> 00:45:44,808
So, perhaps the ingenious and
battle hardened creatures of

342
00:45:44,908 --> 00:45:49,079
the desert might
just inherit the Earth.

343
00:45:55,151 --> 00:45:59,389
In Australia, desert life
shows its remarkable ability

344
00:45:59,489 --> 00:46:02,359
to bounce back.

345
00:46:15,271 --> 00:46:18,408
Creatures like the red
kangaroo have evolved over

346
00:46:18,508 --> 00:46:22,780
countless generations to cope
with the power of the sun.

347
00:46:25,549 --> 00:46:30,687
They thrive in a landscape
with barely any food or water.

348
00:46:32,455 --> 00:46:35,925
And are able to raise a
new generation in conditions

349
00:46:36,025 --> 00:46:38,729
that would kill us.

350
00:46:45,635 --> 00:46:50,307
We don't know exactly what
our warming world will bring.

351
00:46:53,610 --> 00:46:57,514
But, whatever happens, you can
bet that desert animals will

352
00:46:57,614 --> 00:47:01,418
never give up without a fight.

353
00:47:07,057 --> 00:47:08,292
Captioned by Cotter
Captioning Services.

